window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.5","title":"The Bootstrap (Bonus)","goal":"Bootstrap one small sample of five heart rates: get a standard error and a 95% interval without any formula, then compare with the t-interval.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The sample","text":"Five resting heart rates (bpm) from the lesson. The classic formula SE = s / sqrt(n) is our benchmark.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ndata = np.array([62, 70, 68, 75, 65])\nn = len(data)\nse = data.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n)\n\nprint(f'mean = {data.mean():.1f}')\nprint(f's    = {data.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'classic SE = {se:.2f}')","out":"mean = 68.0\ns    = 4.95\nclassic SE = 2.21","figs":[]},{"title":"Resample 5,000 times","text":"Each resample draws 5 values from our data with replacement (the same person can appear twice), then takes the mean. We keep all 5,000 means.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(2025)\nresamples = rng.choice(data, size=(5000, n), replace=True)\nboot_means = resamples.mean(axis=1)\n\nprint('one resample:', resamples[0], ' mean =', resamples[0].mean())\nprint(f'average of boot means = {boot_means.mean():.2f}')","out":"one resample: [68 65 65 70 65]  mean = 66.6\naverage of boot means = 68.04","figs":[]},{"title":"Bootstrap SE and interval","text":"The SD of the bootstrap means is the bootstrap standard error. The 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the means form a 95% percentile interval.","code":"boot_se = boot_means.std(ddof=1)\nlow, high = np.percentile(boot_means, [2.5, 97.5])\n\nprint(f'bootstrap SE = {boot_se:.2f}')\nprint(f'95% percentile interval = ({low:.1f}, {high:.1f})')","out":"bootstrap SE = 1.99\n95% percentile interval = (64.4, 72.0)","figs":[]},{"title":"Compare with the t-interval","text":"With only five values the t-interval has to be wide to be honest. The bootstrap interval is narrower, because resampling cannot invent information a tiny sample does not contain.","code":"t_low, t_high = stats.t.interval(0.95, df=n - 1, loc=data.mean(), scale=se)\n\nprint(f't-interval        = ({t_low:.1f}, {t_high:.1f})')\nprint(f'bootstrap interval = ({low:.1f}, {high:.1f})')\nprint(f'widths: t = {t_high - t_low:.1f}, bootstrap = {high - low:.1f}')","out":"t-interval        = (61.9, 74.1)\nbootstrap interval = (64.4, 72.0)\nwidths: t = 12.3, bootstrap = 7.6","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"hr <- c(62, 70, 68, 75, 65)\nset.seed(2025)\nboot_means <- replicate(5000, mean(sample(hr, replace = TRUE)))\nsd(boot_means)\nquantile(boot_means, c(0.025, 0.975))\nt.test(hr)$conf.int","expect":"bootstrap sd about 1.99; 2.5% and 97.5% quantiles about 64.4 and 72; t.test conf.int 61.85 to 74.15 (Python: 1.99, (64.4, 72.0), (61.9, 74.1))","note":"sample(hr, replace = TRUE) draws a resample of the same size. Bootstrap numbers are close to Python's but not identical; the t-interval is exact."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore, then press the Bootstrap button and tick Perform bootstrapping (Number of samples 5000, Percentile)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / hr.\nBEGIN DATA\n62 70 68 75 65\nEND DATA.\nBOOTSTRAP\n  /SAMPLING METHOD=SIMPLE\n  /VARIABLES INPUT=hr\n  /CRITERIA CILEVEL=95 CITYPE=PERCENTILE NSAMPLES=5000\n  /MISSING USER=EXCLUDE.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=hr\n  /PLOT=NONE\n  /STATISTICS=DESCRIPTIVES.","note":"The Bootstrap button appears on many dialogs. In the output, the Bootstrap table gives the bias, Std. Error and 95% percentile interval for the mean; the ordinary Descriptives table gives the t-interval. Results are close to Python's, not identical."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput hr\n62\n70\n68\n75\n65\nend\nbootstrap mean = r(mean), reps(5000) seed(2025): summarize hr\nestat bootstrap, percentile\nci means hr","note":"estat bootstrap, percentile shows the bootstrap SE and the percentile interval; ci means gives the t-interval. Bootstrap results are close to Python's, not identical."},"try":["Change 5000 resamples to 500 and see how much the bootstrap SE changes between seeds.","Add more heart rates (say ten values) and predict whether the bootstrap and t-intervals move closer together.","Replace the mean with the median (np.median(resamples, axis=1)) and look at its bootstrap SE."]});
